KLIN TODO
----------

Reviewed for Klin 4.0.0 (18 Nov 2026). Items are in priority order within
each section. Completed work is recorded in the ChangeLog.


PLANNED: SECURITY AND CORRECTNESS

* File sessions: store them in a private directory instead of /tmp by
  default, and create session files with O_NOFOLLOW/O_EXCL. The 128-bit random
  session IDs make symlink attacks impractical today, but /tmp is shared.
* Session locking: concurrent requests in the same session can overwrite
  each other's session data (lost updates).
* Disable core dumps in klind (setrlimit RLIMIT_CORE), with a configuration
  option to re-enable them for debugging; dumps can contain session keys and
  user data.
* HTML-escaped output: <%= expr %> prints its value unescaped, so pages that
  echo request data are open to cross-site scripting unless the author
  escapes by hand. Add an escaping output helper (io_html_printf) and an
  escaping output tag; changing <%= %> itself would break existing pages.
* Client-side sessions: move the versioned cookie format from
  encrypt-then-MAC AES-CBC to an AEAD cipher (e.g. AES-GCM).


PLANNED: FEATURES AND TESTING

* HTTP/1.1 request side: chunked request bodies (now rejected with 400) and
  answering "Expect: 100-continue". Persistent connections are done; needs
  careful tests, as it touches the hardened parser.
* Prefork and keep-alive: a worker idle on a kept-alive connection counts as
  busy and may cause another worker to start; consider scoping the busy
  timer to request processing and counting requests (not connections) in
  prefork.max_requests_per_child.
* Streaming file uploads: a per-chunk upload callback, so large uploads do
  not have to be held in memory or a temporary file (important on small
  devices).
* send_enc_deflate sends embedded files as stored, raw deflate data, with
  "Content-Encoding: deflate", which HTTP defines as zlib-wrapped data
  (RFC 9110 8.4.1.2). Browsers accept both; strict clients may not. Store
  zlib data (or send gzip) and change the import format together.
* Code coverage reporting in CI, to show what the test suites miss.
* Longer fuzzing campaigns (the parsers and the page translator have a
  fuzz target; CI runs a short smoke test).
* Platform coverage: *BSD is untested; native Windows does not build (use
  WSL; README, KNOWN LIMITATIONS). Decide whether to port LibU and Klin to
  Windows or remove the OS_WIN code. C++ pages (.klx) are not supported by
  the CMake build.



PLANNED: 5.0.0, REMOVING THE KLONE NAMES

The names KLone used are accepted throughout 4.x (README, RENAMED FROM
KLONE) and go in 5.0.0, the next major release. Announce it in the last
4.x release notes. To remove them:

* delete cmake/klone_compat.cmake and its include and three calls in
  CMakeLists.txt (the KLONE_* settings, the forwarding headers, the
  klone/kloned links);
* delete the code marked "before KLone was renamed Klin": the klone_sid
  cookie, KLONE_CIPHER_KEY, .klone pages and /index.klone, the embedded
  /etc/kloned.conf and the klone_sess_* session file migration
  (git grep -n "before KLone was renamed Klin" lists every place);
* drop the RENAMED FROM KLONE section from README, keeping one line on the
  history, and the compatibility checks in test/run_build_checks.py and
  test/run_server_tests.py;
* decide about the Windows service name (kloned).


POSSIBLE EXTRAS (only if a user needs them)

* HTTP Basic authentication helper for protected pages (only over TLS).
* Printf-style output tag, e.g. <%=fmt arg %>.
* Binary session values (session_bin_set).
* Request/response convenience helpers.
* Performance: faster request_parse for POST data and argument parsing.


DROPPED

* MatrixSSL and CyaSSL backends, OpenSSL TLS-PSK, the Kilt URL module.
* u_sendmail, scheduled ("cron") pages, per-script ulimits.
* Client-side sessions split across several cookies (browsers limit cookie
  size; use server-side sessions for large data).
* KoanLogic website, product and marketing tasks, and the 3.x release
  checklist.
